-- Q1: hard / L4 — GASB 42 capital asset impairment measurement approach
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Ridgecrest operates a municipal parking garage (net book value $6.8 million) that sustained major structural damage from a flood in August 2026. Engineers determine permanent capacity reduction of 40%, and full restoration to original capacity is not feasible. Under GASB 42, evaluate the appropriate impairment measurement approach.', '["Use the restoration cost approach, since physical damage always requires restoration cost measurement", "Use the deflated depreciated replacement cost approach, since the asset''s replacement value has declined", "Use the service units approach, since the physical damage has permanently reduced the asset''s service utility by a measurable percentage", "Use a fair value less costs to sell approach, consistent with FASB impairment guidance for similar assets"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under GASB 42, paragraph 17, when physical damage permanently reduces an asset''s service utility and full restoration is not feasible, the service units approach measures impairment based on the percentage decline in service capacity. The impairment loss equals the net book value multiplied by the 40% permanent reduction ($6,800,000 × 40% = $2,720,000). Choice A is incorrect because the restoration cost approach applies when the government intends to restore the asset to its prior condition, which is not feasible here.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q2: medium / L3 — GASB 87 lessor accounting receivable and deferred inflow
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'Clearwater County owns a building and leases office space to Greenleaf Consulting for 8 years at $72,000 annually, beginning January 1, 2026. The present value of lease payments at the county''s implicit rate totals $463,000. Under GASB 87, what is the most likely effect on the county''s government-wide financial statements at lease commencement?', '["The county removes the building''s carrying value from capital assets and recognizes annual rental revenue on a straight-line basis", "The county recognizes a $463,000 lease receivable and a $463,000 deferred inflow of resources, amortized as lease revenue over the 8-year term", "The county recognizes a $463,000 lease receivable offset by capital asset derecognition and unearned revenue", "The county recognizes $72,000 as lease revenue each year with no balance sheet effect since the building remains a government asset"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under GASB 87, paragraphs 52-53, a government lessor recognizes a lease receivable measured at the present value of lease payments and a corresponding deferred inflow of resources at lease commencement. The deferred inflow is amortized as lease revenue systematically over the lease term. The underlying capital asset is not derecognized. Choice D is incorrect because GASB 87 requires balance sheet recognition of the receivable and deferred inflow rather than simple cash-basis reporting.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q3: easy / L1 — Works of art and historical treasures non-capitalization (GASB 34)
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'Under GASB 34, when may a government elect not to capitalize a collection of works of art or historical treasures?', '["When the collection is held for public exhibition, is protected and preserved, and proceeds from sales are used to acquire other collection items", "When the collection has been fully depreciated under the government''s capitalization policy", "When the collection was donated to the government rather than purchased", "When individual items in the collection each have a value below the government''s capitalization threshold"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB 34, paragraph 27, governments are not required to capitalize collections of works of art and historical treasures if the collection meets three conditions: it is held for public exhibition rather than financial gain, it is protected and preserved, and the government has a policy requiring sale proceeds to be used to acquire other items for the collection. Choice D is incorrect because the exemption relates to the collection''s purpose and preservation policy, not to individual items'' dollar values.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q4: medium / L2 — Interfund services provided and used vs transfers
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Oakdale''s internal service fund provides fleet maintenance to city departments. During fiscal year 2026, the general fund pays $185,000 to the internal service fund for vehicle repairs, which approximates the fair value of services provided. Under GASB 34, how should the general fund classify this $185,000 transaction?', '["As an interfund transfer out, reducing fund balance", "As a capital outlay expenditure for fleet assets", "As a reduction of other financing sources", "As an expenditure for purchased services, similar to a payment to an external vendor"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under GASB 34, paragraph 112, interfund services provided and used are exchange-like transactions reported as revenues and expenditures/expenses in the respective funds, similar to transactions with external parties. The general fund reports the $185,000 as a purchased services expenditure, not as a transfer. Choice A is incorrect because transfers represent nonexchange movements of resources between funds, whereas this transaction reflects a quasi-external exchange of services at approximate fair value.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q5: easy / L2 — Bond proceeds classified as other financing sources
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Westfield issues $12,000,000 in general obligation bonds in its capital projects fund to finance construction of a new fire station. Under governmental fund accounting, how should the bond proceeds be classified?', '["As revenue in the capital projects fund", "As other financing sources in the capital projects fund", "As a long-term liability in the capital projects fund", "As a deferred inflow of resources in the capital projects fund"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under GASB 34, paragraphs 86-87, governmental funds use the current financial resources measurement focus and do not report long-term liabilities. Bond proceeds are classified as other financing sources rather than revenue because they create an obligation rather than an inflow from operations. Choice C is incorrect because governmental funds do not report long-term debt on their balance sheets; the liability appears only in the government-wide statements.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q6: hard / L3 — GASB 68 blended discount rate when plan assets projected insufficient
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Mapleleaf participates in a single-employer defined benefit pension plan. The actuary projects that plan assets will cover benefit payments for the next 18 years but will be fully depleted by year 19. The expected long-term rate of return on plan assets is 7.25%, and the yield on a 20-year AA-rated municipal bond index is 3.85%. Under GASB 68, how does the projected asset depletion affect the discount rate?', '["The actuary must use a blended discount rate applying the 7.25% expected return for the first 18 years and the 3.85% municipal bond rate for benefit payments beyond year 18", "The actuary must use the 3.85% municipal bond rate for all projected benefit payments once depletion is projected in any year", "The actuary must use the 7.25% expected return for all benefit payments because assets are currently sufficient", "The actuary must use the average of the two rates, or 5.55%, applied uniformly to all projected benefit payments"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB 68, paragraph 26, the discount rate is a blended rate when projected plan assets are insufficient to cover all future benefit payments. The expected rate of return is applied to benefits projected to be paid during years when assets are sufficient, while the municipal bond index rate applies to payments in years when assets are projected to be depleted. Choice B is incorrect because the single municipal bond rate applies only to the underfunded projection period, not to the entire benefit stream.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q7: medium / L3 — Lapsing encumbrances year-end effect on fund balance
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The Town of Ashbury has $145,000 in outstanding encumbrances for police equipment at fiscal year-end. The town''s policy treats general fund encumbrances as lapsing. The equipment is delivered the following January for $148,000. What is the most likely effect on the general fund at year-end and in the subsequent year?', '["The $145,000 encumbrance remains on the balance sheet as a liability, and the $148,000 is recorded as an expenditure the following year", "The $145,000 encumbrance is reported as committed fund balance at year-end, and the $148,000 expenditure is charged to prior-year appropriations", "The $145,000 encumbrance is reversed at year-end with no balance sheet effect, and the $148,000 is charged as an expenditure against new-year appropriations", "The $145,000 encumbrance converts to accounts payable at year-end, and the $3,000 difference is recorded the following year"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under GASB 54 and GASB 34 budgetary guidance, lapsing encumbrances are reversed at fiscal year-end because the related appropriations expire. When equipment is delivered in the new year, the $148,000 is charged as an expenditure against new fiscal year appropriations. Choice A is incorrect because encumbrances are budgetary accounts that reserve fund balance, not liabilities, and lapsing encumbrances do not carry forward on the balance sheet.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q8: medium / L3 — Government-wide elimination of interfund balances between activity types
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Southridge''s general fund reports a $1,200,000 interfund receivable from the parks special revenue fund, and the water enterprise fund owes the general fund $800,000 for a separate advance. In preparing the government-wide statement of net position, how should the finance director treat these two interfund balances?', '["Eliminate both balances in full, leaving zero interfund amounts on the government-wide statements", "Eliminate the $1,200,000 between governmental funds and also eliminate the $800,000 because all internal balances must be removed", "Eliminate neither balance because government-wide statements present all fund activity without adjustment", "Eliminate the $1,200,000 between governmental funds, and report the $800,000 as an internal balance between governmental and business-type activities"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under GASB 34, paragraph 58, interfund balances between funds within the same activity type (both governmental) are eliminated in the government-wide statements. However, residual balances between governmental activities and business-type activities are reported as internal balances on separate lines in the statement of net position. Choice A is incorrect because the $800,000 between governmental and business-type activities is presented as an internal balance to preserve the distinction between activity types.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q9: medium / L3 — Enterprise fund capital grant classification as nonoperating
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Riverton''s water utility enterprise fund receives a $2,400,000 state grant to upgrade its wastewater treatment plant. The upgraded facility will serve customers for 30 years. The utility also collects $9,800,000 in water service charges. Under GASB 34, how does the grant''s classification affect the enterprise fund''s operating income?', '["The $2,400,000 is operating revenue because it directly supports service delivery, increasing operating income to $12,200,000 before expenses", "The $2,400,000 is a capital contribution reported below nonoperating items, so operating income reflects only the $9,800,000 in charges minus operating expenses", "The $2,400,000 is deferred and amortized as operating revenue over the 30-year useful life of the upgraded facility", "The $2,400,000 is general revenue reported on the government-wide statement of activities, not on the enterprise fund statements"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under GASB 34, paragraph 100, capital grants and contributions restricted for capital purposes are reported as capital contributions below nonoperating revenues and expenses on the proprietary fund statement. Operating income includes only charges for services and operating grants, not capital contributions. Choice A is incorrect because capital grants for infrastructure acquisition do not qualify as operating revenue; they relate to capital asset construction rather than ongoing operations.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q10: hard / L4 — GASB 51 internally generated intangible asset cost capitalization phases
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'Fairview County''s IT department develops a custom financial reporting application in-house during fiscal year 2026, incurring $85,000 in preliminary project evaluation costs, $340,000 in application development costs (coding, testing, installation), and $48,000 in post-implementation training costs. The application is placed in service November 1, 2026. Under GASB 51, evaluate which costs should be capitalized as an intangible asset.', '["All three cost categories totaling $473,000, since internally generated software includes all project-related outlays", "The $85,000 preliminary evaluation and $340,000 development costs totaling $425,000, since training is a period expense", "Only the $340,000 application development costs, since preliminary and post-implementation costs must be expensed", "The $340,000 development and $48,000 training costs totaling $388,000, since training is part of making the software operational"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under GASB 51, paragraphs 8-10, internally generated computer software follows a three-stage model. Preliminary project stage costs ($85,000) and post-implementation/operation stage costs ($48,000 training) must be expensed as incurred. Only application development stage costs ($340,000 for coding, testing, and installation) qualify for capitalization. Choice B is incorrect because preliminary project evaluation costs are excluded from capitalization regardless of the project''s success.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q11: easy / L1 — Primary objective of governmental financial reporting (GASB Concepts Statement No. 1)
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'Under GASB Concepts Statement No. 1, what is the primary objective of governmental financial reporting?', '["Accountability to citizens, legislative bodies, and oversight agencies", "Providing investors with information to assess profitability and growth potential", "Measuring the government''s net income for performance evaluation", "Reporting earnings per share to bondholders and creditors"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB Concepts Statement No. 1, paragraph 56, the primary objective of governmental financial reporting is accountability, enabling citizens, legislative bodies, and oversight agencies to assess how resources were raised and spent in compliance with laws, budgets, and regulations. Choice B is incorrect because government financial reporting is not designed for investor profitability analysis; governments do not have equity investors seeking return on investment.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q12: medium / L2 — Capital asset useful life revision and prospective depreciation change
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Thornton purchased a water treatment facility in 2019 for $8,400,000 with an estimated useful life of 30 years and no residual value. After 7 years of straight-line depreciation, a 2026 engineering study concludes the facility will last an additional 28 years. Under GASB 62, what is the revised annual depreciation amount beginning in fiscal year 2026?', '["$280,000, continuing the original 30-year depreciation schedule", "$240,000, based on the original cost divided by the new total life of 35 years", "$300,000, based on accelerating depreciation to reflect the engineering study", "$230,000, based on the remaining book value of $6,440,000 divided by 28 years"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under GASB 62, paragraphs 78-80, a change in useful life is a change in accounting estimate applied prospectively. After 7 years, accumulated depreciation is $1,960,000 ($8,400,000 ÷ 30 × 7), leaving a remaining book value of $6,440,000. Dividing by the new remaining life of 28 years yields $230,000. Choice B is incorrect because it improperly divides the original cost by the new total life of 35 years rather than applying the change prospectively to the remaining depreciable amount.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q13: medium / L3 — Enabling legislation creates restricted net position (GASB 46)
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The State of Newburgh collects a 2% hotel occupancy tax under a statute dedicating proceeds exclusively to tourism promotion. The state holds $4,200,000 in unspent hotel tax collections at year-end. The finance director classifies this amount as unrestricted net position, reasoning that the legislature can repeal the statute at any time. What is the most likely effect on the government-wide statement of net position?', '["The classification is correct because legislative restrictions are internal to the government and never create restricted net position", "The classification is incorrect because revenue restricted by enabling legislation must be reported as restricted net position even though the legislature could repeal the statute", "The classification is correct because only federal or court-ordered restrictions qualify as external restrictions on net position", "The classification is incorrect because the amount should be reported as committed net position rather than restricted or unrestricted"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under GASB 46, enabling legislation creates a legally enforceable restriction that qualifies resources as restricted net position on the government-wide statements. Revenue from a statute dedicating hotel taxes to tourism is restricted by enabling legislation regardless of the legislature''s theoretical ability to repeal it. Choice A is incorrect because GASB 46 specifically established that enabling legislation constitutes an external-like restriction classified as restricted net position.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q14: medium / L2 — Voluntary nonexchange transaction with time requirement (GASB 33)
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'On July 1, 2026, the Morrison Foundation pledges $2,000,000 to the City of Lakewood for a community health clinic, specifying that funds may not be spent before January 1, 2028. The foundation transfers the full $2,000,000 on September 1, 2026. The city''s fiscal year ends December 31. Under GASB 33, how should the city report the $2,000,000 in its government-wide financial statements for fiscal year 2026?', '["Report $2,000,000 as a deferred inflow of resources because the time requirement has not been satisfied", "Recognize $2,000,000 as revenue because the cash has been received and eligibility requirements are met", "Recognize $2,000,000 as revenue and simultaneously restrict net position until January 1, 2028", "Report $2,000,000 as a liability owed to the Morrison Foundation until the spending condition is fulfilled"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB 33, paragraph 20, voluntary nonexchange transactions with time requirements are recognized as revenue in the period specified by the provider. Because the Morrison Foundation stipulated that funds may not be spent before January 1, 2028, the $2,000,000 received in 2026 is reported as a deferred inflow of resources until the time requirement is met. Choice B is incorrect because the time requirement is an eligibility condition that delays revenue recognition even though cash was received.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q15: medium / L2 — Capitalization threshold policy for individual assets (GASB 34)
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The Town of Redfield has a capital asset capitalization policy of $25,000 for equipment. During fiscal year 2026, the town purchases four standalone network servers at $18,000 each ($72,000 total), with each server supporting a different department. Under GASB 34, how should Redfield report these purchases in the government-wide statements?', '["Capitalize the full $72,000 as a single data center asset because the combined purchase exceeds the threshold", "Capitalize each server individually at $18,000 because the servers are related-purpose equipment", "Expense the entire $72,000 because each individual server is below the $25,000 capitalization threshold", "Capitalize $50,000 (the amount exceeding the threshold) and expense the remaining $22,000"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under GASB 34, capital assets are evaluated against the government''s capitalization threshold on an individual basis when each unit functions independently. Since each server costs $18,000, which is below Redfield''s $25,000 threshold and each supports a different department, all four are expensed. Choice A is incorrect because grouping independently functioning assets to exceed the threshold is not permitted when each unit operates as a standalone item.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q16: medium / L2 — Escheat property reporting (GASB 37)
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'During fiscal year 2026, the State of Greystone collects $3,800,000 in unclaimed property from abandoned bank accounts, uncashed checks, and dormant safe deposit boxes. The state''s escheat law requires holding the property for potential owner claims with no time limit. Under GASB 37, how should the state report the escheat property?', '["In a private-purpose trust fund because the assets are held for the benefit of individuals outside the government", "In a custodial fund because the state merely serves as a custodian for the original property owners", "As a contingent liability in the notes to the financial statements", "As revenue in the governmental fund entitled to the property, with a liability for amounts estimated to be reclaimed"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under GASB 37, paragraph 2, escheat property is reported as an asset in the governmental or proprietary fund entitled to the property, with revenue recognized and a corresponding liability for amounts expected to be reclaimed by original owners. The government is the legal owner, subject only to potential reclamation claims. Choice A is incorrect because escheat property inures to the government rather than being held in trust for external beneficiaries.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q17: easy / L1 — Only general fund may report positive unassigned fund balance (GASB 54)
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'Under GASB 54, which governmental fund may report a positive unassigned fund balance?', '["Any governmental fund with residual resources", "Only the general fund", "Any fund classified as a major fund", "Both the general fund and special revenue funds"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under GASB 54, paragraph 17, the unassigned classification represents fund balance not constrained for any purpose, and only the general fund may report a positive unassigned fund balance. Other governmental funds may report a negative unassigned balance when expenditures exceed restricted, committed, and assigned amounts, but positive residual amounts must be classified as assigned. Choice D is incorrect because special revenue fund resources are restricted or committed to specific purposes.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q18: hard / L4 — Joint venture equity interest reporting (GASB 14)
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Elmsdale and two neighboring cities each contribute $4,000,000 to create the Tri-City Transit Authority. Each city appoints two of six board members, and the charter grants each city a one-third ongoing financial interest in net position, requiring unanimous consent for dissolution. The Authority''s net position at year-end is $15,600,000. Under GASB 14, evaluate the most appropriate reporting treatment for Elmsdale.', '["Report a $5,200,000 equity interest as a noncurrent asset in the government-wide statements, with note disclosures of the Authority''s separately issued financial statements", "Discretely present the Authority as a component unit because the city appoints one-third of the board", "Blend the Authority into the city''s financial statements because the city has an ongoing financial interest", "Report no asset, recognizing only the $4,000,000 contribution as an expenditure with note disclosure of the joint arrangement"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB 14, paragraph 73, a joint venture is an entity owned by two or more governments that maintains an ongoing financial interest. Each participant reports its equity interest as a noncurrent asset on the government-wide statements. Elmsdale''s one-third share equals $5,200,000 ($15,600,000 × 1/3). Choice B is incorrect because the Authority is not a component unit of any single city since no one government has the ability to impose its will or establish financial accountability.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q19: easy / L1 — General revenues on statement of activities (GASB 34)
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'Under GASB 34, which of the following is classified as a general revenue on the government-wide statement of activities?', '["Charges for services by the parks department", "A federal grant restricted to highway construction", "Unrestricted property tax revenue", "Operating grants for public safety programs"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under GASB 34, paragraph 37, general revenues are all revenues that are not program revenues. Property taxes are always general revenues because they are not directly generated by a specific program. Unrestricted intergovernmental revenues and investment earnings are also general revenues. Choice A is incorrect because charges for services are program revenues directly linked to the function that generates them.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q20: easy / L1 — CAFR three major sections
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'Under GASB 34, a state or local government''s comprehensive annual financial report contains which three major sections?', '["Financial statements, statistical tables, and auditor''s report", "Balance sheets, income statements, and cash flow statements", "Budget documents, management letter, and performance measures", "Introductory section, financial section, and statistical section"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under GASB 34, the comprehensive annual financial report is organized into three sections: the introductory section (letter of transmittal, organizational chart), the financial section (MD&A, basic financial statements, notes, and RSI), and the statistical section (trend data and demographic information). Choice A is incorrect because the auditor''s report is part of the financial section rather than a standalone major section.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q21: hard / L4 — Phase 3 government retroactive infrastructure reporting (GASB 34)
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'Greenbrook Township, a Phase 3 government with revenues below $10 million, is completing its first GASB 34 implementation. The township has major infrastructure acquired over the past 40 years but reliable cost records only for the most recent 15 years. The finance director is evaluating whether to capitalize historical infrastructure retroactively. Under GASB 34, evaluate the township''s infrastructure reporting options.', '["The township must capitalize and depreciate all infrastructure from the past 40 years, estimating historical costs where records are unavailable", "The township may prospectively report only infrastructure acquired or significantly improved after adopting GASB 34 and is encouraged but not required to retroactively capitalize prior infrastructure", "The township must capitalize infrastructure from the past 25 years and may omit assets older than that based on presumed end of useful life", "The township must capitalize all infrastructure and use the modified approach to avoid tracking historical depreciation for older assets"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under GASB 34, paragraphs 148-150, Phase 3 governments (revenues below $10 million) were encouraged but not required to retroactively capitalize major general infrastructure assets. These governments may elect to prospectively report only infrastructure acquired or significantly improved after adopting the reporting model. Choice A is incorrect because GASB 34 recognized cost-benefit concerns of small governments and granted prospective-only reporting as an acceptable alternative.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q22: medium / L3 — Supply inventories under the purchases method in governmental funds
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Grandview''s general fund purchases $380,000 of office supplies on October 1, 2026. By December 31, $280,000 have been consumed, leaving $100,000 in storage. The city uses the purchases method for inventory accounting. What is the most likely effect on the general fund''s reported expenditures and fund balance at year-end?', '["Expenditures of $380,000 are reported at purchase, and the $100,000 remaining inventory is reported as nonspendable fund balance with a corresponding asset", "Expenditures of $280,000 for supplies consumed, and the $100,000 is an inventory asset with no fund balance reclassification", "Expenditures of $380,000 are reported, with no inventory asset or fund balance reclassification because the purchases method expenses all acquisitions", "Expenditures of $280,000 are reported, and the $100,000 is a deferred outflow of resources"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB 54 and GASB 34, the purchases method records the full $380,000 as expenditures when purchased. However, if significant inventories remain at year-end, the inventory must be reported as an asset with an offsetting nonspendable fund balance to indicate those resources are not available for spending. Choice C is incorrect because even under the purchases method, material year-end inventories must be reported on the balance sheet per GASB 54 nonspendable requirements.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q23: medium / L3 — Enterprise fund mandatory reporting criteria (GASB 34 paragraph 67)
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Brookdale operates a municipal golf course charging green fees that cover 45% of operating costs, with the remaining 55% funded by general fund transfers. Debt is secured by general revenues, not by golf course charges. The finance director reports the golf course in the general fund. Under GASB 34, what is the most likely basis for evaluating whether an enterprise fund is required?', '["The golf course must be in an enterprise fund because it charges fees to external users", "The golf course must be in an enterprise fund because it provides services similar to private-sector entities", "The golf course is not required to be in an enterprise fund because none of the three mandatory criteria under GASB 34 paragraph 67 are met", "The golf course is not required to be in an enterprise fund because it operates at a loss"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under GASB 34, paragraph 67, enterprise fund reporting is mandatory only when: (1) debt is secured solely by pledged fees, (2) laws require cost recovery through fees, or (3) pricing policies are designed to recover costs including capital costs. The golf course fails all three because debt is backed by general revenues, no law mandates cost recovery, and fees cover only 45%. Choice A is incorrect because merely charging fees to external users does not trigger mandatory enterprise fund reporting.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q24: medium / L2 — Assigned fund balance authority delegation (GASB 54)
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Belmont''s city council passes an ordinance designating the city manager as the official authorized to assign general fund balance for specific intended uses. On November 15, 2026, the city manager assigns $450,000 for information technology upgrades planned for the next fiscal year. Under GASB 54, what is the status of the $450,000 assignment?', '["Invalid, because only the city council has authority to constrain fund balance under GASB 54", "Valid, because the governing body may delegate the authority to assign fund balance to a body, official, or manager", "Valid only if the city council ratifies the assignment before fiscal year-end", "Invalid, because assigned fund balance requires an accompanying legal restriction under state law"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under GASB 54, paragraph 16, assigned fund balance represents amounts constrained by the government''s intent for a specific purpose. The governing body may delegate the authority to assign fund balance to a finance committee, city manager, or other official through formal action. Since the council passed an authorizing ordinance, the $450,000 assignment is valid. Choice A is incorrect because GASB 54 specifically permits delegation of assignment authority, unlike committed fund balance.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q25: medium / L2 — Encumbrance liquidation when invoice differs from PO amount
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The Town of Pinehurst issued a $260,000 purchase order for fire equipment in its general fund. The equipment is delivered and the actual invoice is $267,500. Under governmental fund accounting, what journal entries are required when the equipment is received?', '["Record expenditures of $260,000 and a payable of $260,000, ignoring the invoice difference", "Record the encumbrance at $267,500 and credit budgetary fund balance reserved for encumbrances at $267,500", "Reverse the encumbrance at $267,500 and record expenditures of $260,000", "Reverse the $260,000 encumbrance and record an expenditure of $267,500 with a corresponding payable"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under GASB 34 budgetary accounting guidance, when goods are received, two entries are required: first, reverse the original encumbrance at the encumbered amount ($260,000), and second, record the actual expenditure at the invoice amount ($267,500) with a corresponding vouchers payable. The encumbrance is always reversed at the original estimated amount. Choice A is incorrect because the expenditure must reflect the actual invoice price of $267,500, not the estimated purchase order amount.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q26: easy / L1 — Fiscal accountability applies to governmental fund statements
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'Under GASB Concepts Statement No. 1, which set of financial statements primarily demonstrates fiscal accountability?', '["Governmental fund financial statements", "Government-wide financial statements", "Proprietary fund financial statements", "Fiduciary fund financial statements"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB Concepts Statement No. 1, paragraph 77, governmental fund financial statements demonstrate fiscal accountability by showing whether resources were raised and spent in compliance with the government''s legally adopted budget and applicable laws. The modified accrual basis and current financial resources measurement focus support this compliance-oriented objective. Choice B is incorrect because government-wide financial statements demonstrate operational accountability using full accrual accounting.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q27: easy / L1 — Other financing sources definition and examples
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'Under governmental fund accounting, which of the following is classified as an other financing source rather than revenue?', '["Property tax collections received within the availability period", "Federal grant receipts satisfying all eligibility requirements", "Proceeds from the issuance of general obligation bonds", "Charges for services collected by a government department"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under GASB 34, paragraph 86, proceeds from issuance of long-term debt are classified as other financing sources in governmental funds because they create an obligation to repay rather than representing earned or nonexchange inflows. Transfers in from other funds are also other financing sources. Choice A is incorrect because property taxes meeting the measurable and available criteria are recognized as revenue, not as other financing sources.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q28: medium / L2 — Special revenue fund revenue source requirement (GASB 54)
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Woodland operates a highway maintenance special revenue fund supported entirely by a dedicated state fuel tax allocation. In January 2027, the state legislature repeals the fuel tax allocation effective immediately. The city''s fiscal year ends June 30, 2027. Under GASB 54, what is the most likely consequence for the highway maintenance fund?', '["The fund continues reporting as a special revenue fund through fiscal year-end because it was properly established", "The fund should be closed or reclassified because the specific revenue source that justified its existence has been eliminated", "The fund automatically converts to a permanent fund to preserve any remaining fund balance", "The fund continues indefinitely as long as it has a positive fund balance from prior years"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under GASB 54, paragraph 30, a special revenue fund accounts for proceeds of specific revenue sources that are restricted or committed to expenditure for specified purposes. When the revenue source is eliminated, the fund no longer meets special revenue criteria and should be closed or reclassified. Choice D is incorrect because a positive fund balance alone does not justify continuing a special revenue fund; the specific restricted or committed revenue source is the defining characteristic.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q29: easy / L1 — Basic financial statements three categories (GASB 34)
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'Under GASB 34, what three categories compose a government''s basic financial statements?', '["Introductory letter, financial statements, and statistical section", "Management''s discussion and analysis, budgetary comparisons, and notes", "Budget documents, general fund statements, and audit report", "Government-wide statements, fund financial statements, and notes to the financial statements"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under GASB 34, paragraph 6, the basic financial statements consist of government-wide financial statements (statement of net position and statement of activities), fund financial statements (governmental, proprietary, and fiduciary), and notes to the financial statements. Choice A is incorrect because the introductory letter and statistical section are CAFR components but not part of the basic financial statements. MD&A is required supplementary information preceding the basic statements.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q30: hard / L4 — GASB 91 conduit debt with extended issuer commitment
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The Westfield Development Authority issues $18,000,000 in revenue bonds on behalf of Greenfield Hospital, a private nonprofit. The bonds are secured solely by hospital patient revenues, but the Authority pledges to cover up to 12 months of debt service from its own reserves if the hospital experiences a revenue shortfall, with maximum exposure of $1,440,000. Under GASB 91, evaluate the appropriate reporting treatment for the Authority.', '["Report the $18,000,000 as long-term debt because the commitment transforms the conduit nature of the arrangement", "Report no conduit debt obligation but disclose the $18,000,000 in the notes as a related-party transaction", "Report the $18,000,000 as a note disclosure for conduit debt issued, and recognize a separate liability for the $1,440,000 commitment to the extent a payment is probable", "Report neither the bonds nor the commitment because the hospital is the primary obligor and the Authority bears no risk"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under GASB 91, paragraphs 8-9, conduit debt obligations are not reported as liabilities of the issuing government because the third party is the primary obligor. However, the issuer must disclose outstanding conduit debt in the notes. When the issuer has made an additional commitment such as a debt service reserve pledge, the commitment is evaluated separately and recognized as a liability when payment is probable. Choice A is incorrect because the additional commitment does not transform the conduit debt into the Authority''s own obligation.', 'hard', 4)
;

COMMIT;
